%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E1F1FC65] ## Frequency response of a transfer function
%[text] A transfer function with complex poles has undamped natural frequencies given by the magnitudes of the poles: $\\omega\_n = |p|${"altText":"\omega\_n = |p|"}. If an input force drives a system at or near its natural frequency, the solution can grow rapidly in magnitude.
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:12b1) **Exercise.** In this exercise, you will visually analyze a mass-spring-damper's response to sinusoidal inputs $u = \\sin( \\omega\_u t )${"altText":"u = \sin( \omega\_u t )"} with various frequencies $\\omega\_u${"altText":"\omega\_u"}.
%[text]  ![mass\_spring\_damper2-flipped.png](text:image:8044)
%[text]  *Mass-spring-damper diagram*
%[text] Consider the mass-spring-damper transfer function with $m = 1${"altText":"m = 1"}, $c = 0.1${"altText":"c = 0.1"}, and $k = 1${"altText":"k = 1"}:
%[text]  $\\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{1}{ s^2 + 0.1 s + 1}${"altText":"\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{1}{ s^2 + 0.1 s + 1}"}
%[text] **(a)** What are the poles of the transfer function?
