%[text] %[text:anchor:H_093EEE7D] ### Constructing a Bode plot
%[text] In general, the frequency response of a transfer function is analyzed by providing input sinusoids with different frequencies and identifying how the magnitude and phase of the steady output changes.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![InputOutputResponse.png](text:image:363a)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *The steady response of a linear time-invariant system to a sine input will be a sine wave with the same frequency as the input. The response sine wave undergoes a phase shift and a change in magnitude that depend on the frequency of the input sine wave. This plot only shows the response to a single frequency.* 
%[text] The frequency-domain analysis is often shown in a **Bode plot**. The Bode plot is constructed by plotting the phase shift and magnitude at varied input frequencies, capturing the system's response to many different frequencies in a single diagram.
%%
%[text]  ![interact.png](text:image:5a9b) **Activity.** In this activity, you will construct the Bode plot for the mass-spring-damper displacement transfer function:
%[text]  $\\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{1}{ s^2 + 0.1 s + 1}${"altText":"\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{1}{ s^2 + 0.1 s + 1}"}
%[text] For each sinusoidal input $u(t) = \\sin(\\omega\_u t)${"altText":"u(t) = \sin(\omega\_u t)"} the output $x(t)${"altText":"x(t)"} shows a transient behavior and a steady state behavior. The steady-state behavior is a sine wave with magnitude $M${"altText":"M"} and phase $\\phi${"altText":"\phi"}: 
%[text]  $x\_{steady}(t) = M \\sin (\\omega\_u t + \\phi )${"altText":"x\_{steady}(t) = M \sin (\omega\_u t + \phi )"}
%[text] The Bode plot is generated by plotting the magnitude and phase of the steady-state output against the input frequency, $\\omega\_u${"altText":"\omega\_u"}. The magnitude and frequency are plotted on logarithmic axes. 
%[text] **Part 1.** Start by running this section to create the figure and the transfer function `G`.
G = tf([0 0 1],[1 0.1 1]); % The transfer function
fh = figure("Position",[1 1 1500 700]); % Create figure with handle fh
  %[control:button:1bd5]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] **Part 2.**
%[text] 1. Set a frequency `omegau` for the input sine wave.
%[text] 2. Observe the phase and magnitude of the steady response. Observe that the phase and magnitude are automatically added as a new point on the Bode diagram.
%[text] 3. Repeat until the Bode diagram figure is populated. \
omegau = 3; % Input frequency in rads/second %[control:slider:61d1]{"position":[10,11]}
omegau = omegau; % If you prefer to type values rather than use the slider, do so here.
createBodePlot(fh,omegau,G);
%[text] ![brightbulb.png](text:image:71b9) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - At which frequency is the most significant change in magnitude observed?
%[text] - At which frequencies are the oscillations attenuated?
%[text] - How does the phase of the response change between low and high frequency input signals? \
%%
