%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F9BFDD20] ## Numerical evaluation of the frequency response
%[text] It is not necessary to compute the time-domain response to create a Bode plot as done in the activity above. The activity only illustrates the principle behind the Bode plot. In practice, the frequency response can be computed entirely in the $s${"altText":"s"}-domain. This is accomplished by:
%[text] 1. generating the *complex transfer function* by making the substitution $s = i \\omega${"altText":"s = i \omega"}
%[text] 2. evaluating the complex transfer function at various frequencies
%[text] 3. computing the magnitude and phase of the result \
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:34cf) **Example.** Generate a Bode plot for the mass-spring-damper system:
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{1}{ s^2 + 0.1 s + 1}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{1}{ s^2 + 0.1 s + 1}"}
%[text] by evaluating the complex transfer function.
%[text] **Solution.** 
%[text] The complex transfer function is created by setting $s = i \\omega${"altText":"s = i \omega"}:
%[text]  $G(i \\omega) = \\frac{1}{  (i\\omega)^2 + 0.1(i  \\omega) + 1} = \\frac{1}{ - \\omega^2 + 0.1 i  \\omega + 1}${"altText":"G(i \omega) = \frac{1}{  (i\omega)^2 + 0.1(i  \omega) + 1} = \frac{1}{ - \omega^2 + 0.1 i  \omega + 1}"}
%[text] Numerically evaluating the complex transfer function for many frequencies $\\omega${"altText":"\omega"} can be accomplished using an array of frequencies.
omega = linspace(0.1,10,500);                   % An array of frequencies
Giomega = 1./(-omega.^2 + 0.1*1i*omega + 1)     % Complex transfer function evaluated at many frequencies
%[text] The magnitude and phase of complex transfer function values can be computed directly on the arrays.
M = abs(Giomega);       % The magnitude of the complex number
Mdb = 20*log10(M)       % Magnitude in dB
phi = angle(Giomega);   % Phase angle in radians
phiDeg = phi*180/pi     % Phase angle in degrees
%[text] The first panel in the Bode plot is constructed by graphing the magnitude in dB against the input frequencies, $\\omega${"altText":"\omega"}.
figure
semilogx(omega,Mdb)
xlabel("Frequency (rad/s)")
ylabel("Magnitude (dB)")
%[text] The second panel is constructed by graphing the phase in degrees against the input frequencies, $\\omega${"altText":"\omega"}.
figure
semilogx(omega,phiDeg)
xlabel("Frequency (rad/s)")
ylabel("Phase (degrees)")
%%
