%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4417F3AF] ## Buck converter analysis
%[text] In an electric vehicle, the battery must power high voltage loads, like the DC motor (~600V) and low voltage loads, like the headlights (~12V). The battery bank will typically produce voltages in the range required by the DC motor. So how do you efficiently reduce the voltage so that it can power the low voltage systems?
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![dcdc\_highlight.png](text:image:4d33)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Simplified electric vehicle schematic*
%[text] An obvious answer to reducing voltage is to connect a resistor in series with the load. This solution is inefficient, as the resistor gives off dissipated energy as heat. A much more practical solution is a switching buck converter. A switching buck converter typically uses a MOSFET (metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistor) to switch the power supply on and off at a high frequency. The average of the resulting voltage will be lower than the original, and the mean value of the output voltage can be changed by altering the duty cycle.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![switchingCircuit.png](text:image:97ff)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *A simple switching circuit. The DC input* $V\_{in}${"altText":"V\_{in}"} is constant. The output voltage $V\_{out}${"altText":"V\_{out}"} depends on the switching characteristics of the MOSFET.
%[text] In the plot above, the duty cycle is $d = 0.5${"altText":"d = 0.5"}, implying that the average output voltage is half the input voltage: $\\bar{V}\_{out} = 0.5 V\_{in}${"altText":"\bar{V}\_{out} = 0.5 V\_{in}"}. A shorter duty cycle will further reduce the mean voltage. The issue with this switching device is that the output is not a DC source: it contains harmonics at the switching frequency. These harmonics can produce undesirable effects in the downstream load. Buck converters typically have a filter to attenuate the switching harmonics.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![filterCircuit.png](text:image:4eea)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *A filter is included in buck converters to attenuate switching harmonics.*
%[text] In this section, you will analyze filter circuits using transfer functions. All of the components are treated as ideal (lossless).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C15A2CAD] ### Inductor filter
%[text] An inductor filters out switching harmonics by storing and releasing energy during the switching cycle.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![filterCircuitInductor.png](text:image:45c6)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *An inductor filter. The diode is required so that current can flow when the MOSFET is off.*
%[text] When the MOSFET is on, current flows from the battery charging the inductor and powering the load. When the MOSFET is off, energy stored in the inductor is released, powering the load.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![filterCircuitCycle.png](text:image:0f65)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Cycle of a switching circuit with an inductor filter. When the MOSFET is off, the inductor powers the load.*
%[text] The filter circuit differential equations are derived using Kirchhoff's voltage law, which states that the voltage around any closed loop of the circuit must be zero. While these two states can be thought of as separate circuits, they can be modeled by a single application of Kirchhoff's voltage law if the potential differences across the diode, voltage source, and switch are all modeled together by a single function, $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"}. In this case, $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"} is a square wave that switches between the battery voltage $V\_{in}${"altText":"V\_{in}"} and zero (the voltage drop across an ideal diode). 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![ut.png](text:image:717d)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *The input function* $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"} *models the potential differences from the voltage source, MOSFET, and diode in one function.*
%[text] With this definition, Kirchhoff's voltage law implies:
%[text]  $u(t) - L \\frac{di}{dt} - iR = 0${"altText":"u(t) - L \frac{di}{dt} - iR = 0"}
%%
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:6024) **Exercise.** Solve for the transfer function of the inductor filter circuit with input $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"}, output $i(t)${"altText":"i(t)"}, and zero initial conditions. 
syms L R s
% Record your expression here in UoverI in terms of the symbolic variables
UoverI = NaN;

check8(UoverI) % Checks your result
%%
%[text] In this analysis, the voltage across the resistor is the variable of interest rather than the current. The current is related to the voltage across the resistor $V${"altText":"V"} by Ohm's law: $V(s) = R I(s)${"altText":"V(s) = R I(s)"}. This implies the voltage transfer function is:
%[text]  $\\frac{V(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{R}{L s + R}${"altText":"\frac{V(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{R}{L s + R}"}
