%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6AC5B55E] ### LC filter
%[text] A single inductor is not typically used to filter a buck converter. A more typical setup includes an inductor and a capacitor.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![filterLC.png](text:image:1120)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Buck converter with an inductor and capacitor (LC) filter.*
%[text] The differential equations that govern this circuit can be derived by applying Kirchhoff's voltage law twice. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![filterLCLoopsV2.png](text:image:32a5)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Loops and assigned current directions for the application of Kirchhoff's laws.*
%[text] The potential differences resulting from the DC source, MOSFET, and diode are all captured in the input function, $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"}. Applying Kirchhoff's voltage law around the two closed loops gives:
%[text]     (1) $u(t) - L \\frac{di}{dt} - i\_2 R = 0${"altText":"u(t) - L \frac{di}{dt} - i\_2 R = 0"}                   (2)  $\\frac{1}{C} \\int  i\_1 d\\tau - i\_2 R = 0${"altText":"\frac{1}{C} \int  i\_1 d\tau - i\_2 R = 0"}
%[text] Taking the Laplace transform yields
%[text]     (1) $U(s) - Ls I(s) - R I\_2(s) = 0${"altText":"U(s) - Ls I(s) - R I\_2(s) = 0"}         (2) $\\frac{1}{Cs} I\_1(s) - R I\_2(s) = 0${"altText":"\frac{1}{Cs} I\_1(s) - R I\_2(s) = 0"}
%[text] Kirchhoff's current law implies that $I  = I\_1 + I\_2${"altText":"I  = I\_1 + I\_2"}. So (1) can be rewritten as
%[text]  $U - Ls(I\_1 + I\_2) - R I\_2 = 0${"altText":"U - Ls(I\_1 + I\_2) - R I\_2 = 0"}
%[text] The variable of interest is $V(s)${"altText":"V(s)"}, the Laplace transform of the voltage across load. By Ohm's law, the voltage drop across the resistor is $V(s) = R I\_2(s)${"altText":"V(s) = R I\_2(s)"}. Substituting this expression and (2) into (1) and simplifying yields
%[text]  $U - Ls(CsV + V/R) - V = 0${"altText":"U - Ls(CsV + V\/R) - V = 0"}
%[text] Rearrange to find the transfer function $V/U${"altText":"V\/U"}:
%[text]  $\\frac{V}{U} = \\frac{R}{LCRs^2 + Ls + R${"altText":"\frac{V}{U} = \frac{R}{LCRs^2 + Ls + R"}
%[text] Notice that in the case where $C = 0${"altText":"C = 0"}, this reduces to the transfer function for the inductor only.
%%
